e error constant)\nbefore performing monthNumber > 12 || monthNumber < 1; keep existing checks for\nmonthString.length and range afterward to ensure invalid numeric parsing is\nrejected early.\n\nIn `@src/utils/isMasterCardNumber.ts`:\n- Around line 1-5: isMasterCardNumber currently crashes on empty input and only\nchecks 51–55 BINs; add guards for empty array and short card strings (ensure\ncardNumbers[0] exists and has sufficient length) and extend detection to include\n2-series BINs by parsing the leading digits: keep the existing check for first\ndigit '5' and second digit between 1 and 5 for 51–55, and also parse the first\nfour digits (or first six if you prefer) as a number and return true when that\nprefix is between 2221 and 2720; reference the isMasterCardNumber function, the\ncardNumbers parameter and the existing secondNumber logic when updating the\nchecks.\n\nIn `@src/utils/isMonthMatch.ts`:\n- Around line 2-3: The current isMonthMatch logic lets non-numeric two-char\nstrings pass because Number('ab') is NaN; change the validation in isMonthMatch\nto first assert the input is exactly two digits (e.g., /^\\d{2}$/ or a digit-only\nlength check on value) and only then convert to a number and verify it is\nbetween 1 and 12 (use parseInt or Number and explicit numeric range checks);\nupdate the checks around the existing \"value\" handling so non-digit\ntwo-character inputs (like 'ab') fail before any numeric comparison occurs.\n\nIn `@src/utils/isVisaCardNumber.ts`:\n- Around line 1-6: The isVisaCardNumber function can throw if cardNumbers is\nempty and currently may return undefined; add an input guard to ensure\ncardNumbers is an array with at least one non-empty string (e.g., check\nArray.isArray(cardNumbers) and cardNumbers.length > 0 and typeof cardNumbers[0]\n=== 'string' and cardNumbers[0].length > 0) and then return a boolean explicitly\n(true if the first character === '4', otherwise false) so the function always\nreturns a boolean value.\n```\n\n